Z||000032||BJW||11-16-2000||03:54 AM||||Funny abouut the SpiralMax . . . <BR>I didn't know about the SpiralMax product and was already designing a device to slightly pressurize the intake . . . using what are called 'muffin fans' . . . <BR>Muffin fans are used in computer intallations to cool large assemblies and they have a fairly large flow rate. Due to their design, in the shape of a venturi with the motor 'faired in' in the center, they probably would not restrict the flow much if not activated, but my idea was to run 3 or 4 bolted together and have at least one running at all times (they are designed for continuous use and are available in 12 volt models)<BR>The other 2 or three would be activated with a throttle switch or switches in stages . . . <BR>I think that in view of the increase in performance with the SpiralMax, my device would at least slightly pressurize the intake as well . . . <BR>I had also thought of using a couple of 'squirrel cage' blowers mounted in tandem (like heater blowers) . . . to do the same thing, and leaving them on at anything over idle speeds . . . <BR>I wonder . . . <BR>the cost of the parts would be minimal for either method . . . might be worth a try.<BR>My '87 GT needs a couple of more horses and I really don't want an engine transplant yet (or NITROUS!)<BR>B. in Toronto||||reg||1
Z||000033||DaRkLoRD||11-16-2000||01:46 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BJW:<BR><B>Funny abouut the SpiralMax . . . <BR>I didn't know about the SpiralMax product and was already designing a device to slightly pressurize the intake . . . using what are called 'muffin fans' . . . <BR>Muffin fans are used in computer intallations to cool large assemblies and they have a fairly large flow rate. Due to their design, in the shape of a venturi with the motor 'faired in' in the center, they probably would not restrict the flow much if not activated, but my idea was to run 3 or 4 bolted together and have at least one running at all times (they are designed for continuous use and are available in 12 volt models)<BR>The other 2 or three would be activated with a throttle switch or switches in stages . . . <BR>I think that in view of the increase in performance with the SpiralMax, my device would at least slightly pressurize the intake as well . . . <BR>I had also thought of using a couple of 'squirrel cage' blowers mounted in tandem (like heater blowers) . . . to do the same thing, and leaving them on at anything over idle speeds . . . <BR>I wonder . . . <BR>the cost of the parts would be minimal for either method . . . might be worth a try.<BR>My '87 GT needs a couple of more horses and I really don't want an engine transplant yet (or NITROUS!)<BR>B. in Toronto</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I'm not sure those fans would move enough air to make a difference.. plus, you'd need to consider moisture in the intake. Another concern would be turbulence in the flow if the fans were at different positions when they started spinning. It might not make a big difference though, if they were fast enough. Don't let me stop you from trying this though!! I think it's a cool idea if it works..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>I found a 5" vent fan which pushes 1,280 CFM.. I'm pretty sure that'd do something to the intake..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> The fan was $60 from Home Hardware, I might buy one to experiment with my ram air intake. <P>Does anyone know how much air the 2.8 takes in at WOT, at the peak of the HP curve? (I hope that makes sense..)<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||1
Z||000034||Raydar||11-16-2000||05:12 PM||||Trav,<BR>Yeah, call me or send e-mail. I'd like to try to make it out there. <BR>We ought to order some Fire Injectors (Do they still make those?) from J.C. Whitney, to throw in to the mix.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Raydar||||reg||6
Z||000035||DaRkLoRD||11-16-2000||05:16 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Raydar:<BR><B>We ought to order some Fire Injectors (Do they still make those?) from J.C. Whitney, to throw in to the mix.  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>what are they?<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||5
Z||000036||Raydar||11-17-2000||10:09 AM||||Fire Injectors were "surface-gap" sparkplugs.<BR>Had a ring of 6 or 8 electrodes that went all the way around the base. The center electrode was pretty much flush with the ground electrodes. Looked like a * .<BR>J.C.Whitney made all sorts of outlandish MPG and HP claims. <BR>(I guess they <I>don't</I> make 'em any more.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> )<BR>I keep wanting to say that 2-stroke outboard motors of the era (mid 60's) used this type of sparkplugs. Not sure about that part, tho'. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar<P>"You want me to do <I>what</I>!? <BR>If I could do <I>that</I> I'd never leave the house!"<p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 11-17-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000037||LAMBO||11-17-2000||01:20 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/smokey.jpg"> <P>Good for 250hp, 223ft/lbs torque AND 49 mpg without a computer. This was done and tested on more than one vehicle (one of them an 84 Fiero) 16 years ago. For you v6 guys, thats a schematic of a stock Iron Duke. Only a cam change was done to the motor. Can somebody build this for me, please. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>TL ||||reg||1
Z||000038||1FST2M6||11-17-2000||03:25 PM||||precooled charge air instead of cooled after it's compressed when it's hotter?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif">  ||||reg||1
Z||000039||Phil||11-17-2000||05:17 PM||||Don't really want to stop anybody from experimenting but muffin fans are usually rated in "free air". If you start to increase the static pressure their cfm drops like a rock and will only develope about 1-2 in. of water "boost" max. (1 psi=about 28in H2O). There is an electric supercharger gadget that gives about 1 lb boost but I think that it draws about 40 amps. It takes quite a bit of power to compress air. ||||reg||1
Z||000040||alpha||11-17-2000||05:40 PM||||On the subject of leaf blower superchargers, spiral max and that sort of stuff, has anyone considered playing with compressed air to provide a boast?<P>If you used a cheap compressed air tank and ran a hose/jet/fitting to the intake do you think you would get noticable hp gains? Or would you cook your engine?<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://fiero.dhs.org/fiero/sig.jpg">   <BR>   <A HREF="mailto:douglas_knight@umit.maine.edu">douglas_knight@umit.maine.edu</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000041||DaRkLoRD||11-17-2000||05:54 PM||||86SE mentioned this a few months ago, I think it was determined that in order to produce any noticable power, the air tank would either need to be huge, or would run out in a couple of seconds.<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||3
Z||000042||theogre||11-17-2000||07:23 PM||||That big image above about the hot air motor..... is that from the article that every one was talking about in another thread..... That engine setup was also presented in Pop Mech or Pop Sci, maybe both, years ago. I thought that whole thing sounded familliar.<P>The whole idea was to get the intake charge up to some rediculously high temp before it hit the cylinders. I could never see how it ran w/o lighting the f/a mix in the intake system. Gasoline mixes flash over at some pretty low temps. Mix must be really lean or something.||||reg||1
Z||000043||LAMBO||11-18-2000||09:41 AM||||theogre, I pulled the article from Hotrodarchives. You have to throw out all preconceptions about how induction is supposed to work. Smokey Yunick drove this car (84 Fiero) for a year with this setup with no problems. Its an amazingly simple set up with everything easily buildable if you know how to weld up water tight boxes. I pulled the patent from the net and it has all the details and schematics on how to build. Patent expires in June of next year.||||reg||1
Z||000044||theogre||11-18-2000||01:36 PM||||If he's still got any value in it he can extend it. There's a couple ways it can be done. Computer industry uses them all the time to prevent competition.<P>what was the patent # on that?<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 11-18-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000045||horseman||11-19-2000||08:50 PM||||Great work Lambo,<BR>I was wondering if anyone would read the article and take interest(re:Smokey's induction setup). I would be very interested in how to axcess the patent info. Please email the link to me. I just happen to have some good friends that might be able to help us. One is a mechanical engineer, two are machinists/hotrodders and another is quite the welder. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000046||mrfixit58||11-20-2000||09:16 AM||||WOW! Someone actually remembers that article except for me! I scanned and sent copies of that article out to a couple of people last year. It was originally published in Hot Rod magazine in 198(4?). I still have the magazine. I remember I was in college and wrote a paper on the article for my thermo-dynamics class. Got a "A" on the paper, too.<P>I always thought that a SBC with 600+HP that got close to 50 MPG would be COOL!<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000047||Raydar||11-20-2000||10:47 AM||||I remember those articles, too. Sounded feasible to my un-engineering-educated mind. (Say that three times, quickly.) <BR>Always wondered why it was never pursued. <BR>After a while, I just figured that it was just another "gee-whiz" device that would never see production.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Raydar<P>"You want me to do <I>what</I>!? <BR>If I could do <I>that</I> I'd never leave the house!"||||reg||6
Z||000048||LAMBO||11-20-2000||02:48 PM||||The article stated he contracted with Edlebrock to go into production with these units and it was never heard from since. It's funny how these type of things seem to just go away. We couldn't possibly have our cake (1.8 hp per cubic inch) and eat it too (48+ mpg). It would be a conflict of interest with OPEC and Detroit. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>TL||||reg||1
Z||000049||bHooper||11-20-2000||03:47 PM||||does anyone have that article, that they could email me?  it looks very interesting.<P>thanks!<P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000050||DaRkLoRD||11-20-2000||04:17 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by LAMBO:<BR><B>The article stated he contracted with Edlebrock to go into production with these units and it was never heard from since. It's funny how these type of things seem to just go away. We couldn't possibly have our cake (1.8 hp per cubic inch) and eat it too (48+ mpg). It would be a conflict of interest with OPEC and Detroit.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>TL</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>it's all about money.. anyone who thinks engines are as efficient as they can be... that's not true. they are extremely inefficient.... but as long as we're willing to pay so much for gas, and put up with so much pollution.. engine designs aren't going to change. I personally prefer fuel injection to carbs, but fuel injection isn't as efficient as a carbeurator can be. A lot of people won't like more efficient engines.. the feeling of being pushed back into your seat when you stomp the gas, is your engine producing excess power.. it'd be more efficient to only use enough fuel to increase your vehicle to the desired speed. no tire squealing, no engine screaming.. not as fun. but you save a lot on gas, and the car makes less pollution. but it's not as fun. hehe..<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||11
Z||000051||horseman||11-20-2000||08:49 PM||||Hooper,<BR>You can find it on hotrodarchives.com(june of '84). It is just like like lambo says. If I remember right, I think Crane was going to release it. I called them and they didn't have a clue. I am pretty sure that they manufactured the camshaft for it though. From an earlier thread I understand that the fiero engine and others may be lying in the back of Smokey's garage. Unfortunately, Smokey is said to be in poor health(enphysema?). It would be a shame if he took the knowledge to the grave with him!<P>Charlie||||reg||1
